6 40 it is wednesday  time for morning cup of joe with metro  sheriff joe lombardo good morning  sheriff it's great to have you  good i'm looking at myself on camera i'm  a little casual today  yeah casual wednesday looks good all  right sheriff  we know you just tell the state of the  department what are some of the  department's goals for this new year  yeah we did unfortunately we couldn't do  it in a public venue because of cobit  obviously  so we did it virtually similar to the  governor's state of the  state and i think it went over well  hopefully  you've had a chance to watch it but in  the previous years  i have concentrated on violent crime as  our priorities and then last year  included quality of life on top of  violent crime and i think i'm going to  continue in that vein will  not think i know i'm going to continue  in that vein moving forward to 2021  violent crime and quality of life and  you know and through the state of  department we talked about  the department's response to covid last  year and you know coming into the 21  and how we dealt with that and  unfortunately we've had over 1200  employees infected with covid and  but we've been pretty successful and as  essential  employees or essential folks within the  community in responding to crime  dealing with those numbers unfortunately  we had  two employees succumb to covid  and i'm pleasantly surprised how we  dealt with it going forward  we developed a new strategic plan um and  the strategic plan is basically our  blueprint  on how we conduct ourselves on a daily  basis and and what our goals are  looking into the future and and how  we're going to fight crime which is our  mission  um as a police department uh we  talked about police reform you know the  stuff we went through in 2020 and  i anticipate we're going to have to deal  with at this legislative session here in  21  all the things associated with the  communities clamoring for police reform  and doing business better  i'm very proud that we have minimal  changes  in the way we do business here at metro  because a lot of those things were  brought forward  in years past uh you know things like  de-escalation and duty to render aid and  and limiting the choke hold uh those  type of items  and then we talked about the budget uh  you know  just like everybody else has gone  through and what las vegas as a whole is  experiencing  how do we deal with the budget  shortfalls um did i have to lay off  employees i unfortunately i had to lay  off some civilian employees  we we got rid of all our part-time  employees we got rid of a couple  specialized programs and we had to  downsize a significant number of  programs  throughout the police department  hopefully through 21 and 22  23 we can recover uh some of those  programs that are essential in what we  do in the police business  so moving forward to 21 homelessness and  mental health  that along with violent crime and that  quality of life is homeless  mental health how we respond to a police  department how we  help the community develop resources in  response to those two particular issues  uh so those are going to be on the  forefront of what we deal with in 21.  you know sheriff speaking of violent  crime according to metro stats homicides  doubled  just last month compared to the same  time last year what do detectives  believe is behind that increase  well fortunately it's only been four  weeks you know  the same time last year so if you move  into february  and year to date we're actually flat  so unfortunately those numbers have  recovered  from the previous year so we were  doubled up in january  and there was no trend identified  you know we've seen we've seen an  increase in violent crime across the  board  across the united states in 2020 and a  lot of  there could be a lot of reasons for that  prison reform  bail reform prosecution  inability because of the closure of the  courts  covet anxiety there's a lot of people  out there theorized  associated with that but my goal is just  to address it  and i think we did a robust job i know  we did a robust job  in addressing that particular issue uh  because we were down  four percent in violent crime compared  to last year the thing i'm most proud of  though is our homicide detail  our homicide investigation our  solvability was right around 92 percent  and if you look at the national average  it's around 62 percent  so that's a lot of lives in that 30  percent delta  associated with that number and and i  think we are the industry standard best  practice  agency uh associated with those type of  investigations  yeah 92 that's really that's great uh  sheriff we know like everything else  super bowl weekend was a lot different  this year  from metro standpoint were there less  issues in terms of dui  no there was less issues as far as crowd  sizes and dealing with crowds and things  that go along with crowds such as  uh you know fighting and as you can  imagine  i don't need to uh tell you about that  but the uh what we did have an increase  was  uh dui enforcement we didn't do check  points this year  we find out those are very limiting in  their in their results  and so we did a lot of saturation  patrols  so we had a 60 increase in dui  arrests and we had a 84 increase  in citations issued so we uh we were  very busy  on super bowl weekend associated with  dui  and the reason why we uh i'm actually  proud of that number because  duis more often than not result in a  fatal accident  and if we can get those individuals off  the roadway and prevent a fatal accident  the better we all are yeah and  unfortunately we report on that far too  often so  good to hear those stats all right  sheriff joe lombardo thank you so much  for joining us  hope you're doing well you too alex  thank you thank you and if you have a  question for sheriff joe lombardo you  can send a test  just go on our website newsnow.com we do  our morning cup of just segments every  wednesday so that would be when your  question is answered  all right

